# Task 5 Report — Causal O'Neil Pyramiding

## Outcome

Implemented strategy-universe Task 5 on branch `codex/pit-optimizer-v5-architecture` from accepted
base `561bd37`.

The source change is limited to:

- `core/backtest_engine.py`
- `core/pit_optimizer_v5/diagnostics.py` (minimal scope expansion explicitly authorized after the
  accepted single-fill endpoint invariant blocked correct weighted-average add-on evidence)

No tests, Task-4 policy files, provider/Docker paths, bundle artifacts, replay, qualification,
deployment, market-data files, push, or merge were changed.

## Pending add-on lifecycle

`PendingAddOn` is the exact frozen/slotted six-field record from the brief. It validates canonical
symbol/date, positive finite risk, optional unit notional cap, and reason text; it has a strict
primitive checkpoint round trip. Pending add-ons and their outcome counters are emitted and
restored only in checkpoint schema V5. Schema-4/V2 payloads remain free of add-on fields.

At every V5 open the engine now:

1. applies authenticated identity transitions and remaps entries, exits, and add-ons;
2. applies opening-gap stops and cancels affected add-ons;
3. cancels any add-on conflicting with a pending full exit, then executes that exit;
4. executes pending entries and then surviving add-ons at an exact eligible Open; and
5. applies intraday stops.

A missing exact Open leaves an add-on pending for the next eligible session. A present but invalid
Open terminates it as `invalid_price`. A stopped/exited holding is cancelled before BUY, and a
missing holding terminates as `no_position`. Duplicate restored/remapped pending symbols fail
closed. The close phase queues at most one add-on per surviving symbol, and the engine bounds each
episode to two add-ons.

The V5 add-on outcome map contains the required exact categories: `queued`, `executed`,
`cancelled`, `already_pending`, `invalid_price`, `risk`, `cash`, `cap`, and `no_position`.

## V3 trusted policy boundary

The run accepts policy interface 2 or 3. V3 is allowed only with V5 execution and an authenticated
schema-V3 PIT bundle. Raw built-in fact extraction stays on the unchanged V2 baseline; every
candidate-facing call is rebuilt at the engine boundary with `StrategyPolicyAdapterV3` and the
causal Task-3 feature builders. Entry, capacity, allocation, eviction, add-on, and exit receive only
their immutable V3 snapshots. The original V2 `ticker in self._open_positions: continue` signal
path is unchanged.

Portfolio snapshots derive cash, gross exposure, drawdown, per-position open risk, pending-entry
competition, and complete industry/unclassified-sector notionals from engine-owned amounts. The
policy interface version is sealed into result configuration and checkpoint fingerprint identity.

## Execution authority and accounting

At the next eligible open, the engine—not candidate policy—computes expected spread, impact, and
commission affordability. It enforces:

- cash and no leverage;
- the 1% engine position-risk ceiling and the requested incremental risk;
- current risk already carried to the unchanged protective stop;
- the optional candidate aggregate-position cap;
- the engine aggregate-position cap derived from the canonical risk/stop contract; and
- the maximum two add-ons per episode.

The actual `apply_friction()` BUY fill supplies execution price and cash delta. The engine updates
remaining/aggregate quantity, remaining-lot weighted-average entry, peak-entry anchor, commission
P&L, and episode state without lowering the existing stop. It records a transaction action `ADD`,
a canonical BUY fill with `add_on:<reason_code>`, and an `add_on` quantity-path transition. This
keeps the V5 fill schema and cash/friction reconciliation unchanged while making pyramiding
distinct in transactions and episode evidence.

## Diagnostics compatibility root cause and fix

The first direct add-on/close smoke exposed an accepted evaluator invariant that required
`episode.entry_price == closed_trade.entry_price == first BUY execution price`. Correctly updating
the trade to a weighted-average entry made every real multi-BUY episode fail before quantity-path
reconciliation.

The authorized diagnostics change replaces only the last equality with the remaining-lot basis
replayed from canonical fills. Each BUY combines its actual execution value with the then-live
quantity and basis; each SELL reduces live quantity without changing the surviving shares'
per-share basis. The existing endpoint checks still bind the first BUY's session and symbol to the
episode, and the first quantity-path row still binds its exact execution price and reason. With one
BUY, the replayed basis equals that same first execution price, so single-fill/V2 behavior is
unchanged. Fill friction, cent rounding, cash totals, ordered quantity changes, BUY/SELL actions,
and terminal quantity reconciliation remain intact.

## P1 remaining-lot basis correction

Review found that an add-on following a scale-out used every historical BUY fill in its numerator
while using only unsold shares in its live quantity. A deterministic pre-fix reproduction (entry
100 at 100, scale out 50 at 120, then add 25.75 at 110) produced engine basis `102.0477137177`
instead of the remaining-lot basis `103.3993399340`.

The focused correction removes the historical-fill scan. Add-on execution now values the existing
position as `trade.entry_price * current_qty` and combines that amount with the new actual fill over
`current_qty + add_qty`. Realized scale-out P&L is left untouched; the unchanged stop and live
quantity continue to be the inputs to engine-owned pre-fill risk sizing. Diagnostics mirrors this
contract by replaying live quantity and basis across the ordered BUY/SELL fill path, rather than
averaging shares already sold. No checkpoint, transaction, fill, or V2 schema changed.

